<a href="#shader-material-uniforms" class="tracking-light my-6 mt-8 block text-balance text-3xl font-bold text-on-surface no-underline hover:underline"><h2 id="shader-material-uniforms">Shader material uniforms</h2></a>
<p class="my-4"><code class="bg-surface-container-high text-on-surface rounded-sm px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono">ShaderMaterial</code>, <code class="bg-surface-container-high text-on-surface rounded-sm px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono">RawShaderMaterial</code> and subclasses keep the material&#x27;s <a href="https://threejs.org/docs/#ShaderMaterial.uniforms" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-primary underline underline-offset-3 hover:decoration-2 [&amp;_code]:text-primary"><code class="bg-surface-container-high text-on-surface rounded-sm px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono">uniforms</code> object</a> stable. When you pass a new <code class="bg-surface-container-high text-on-surface rounded-sm px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono">uniforms</code> object, React Three Fiber merges the incoming uniforms into the existing target instead of replacing it.</p>
<p class="my-4">This preserves three.js internal uniform caching, even if your component creates a fresh <code class="bg-surface-container-high text-on-surface rounded-sm px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono">uniforms</code> object on each render. Existing uniform entries are updated in place and new entries are added as needed.</p>
<div class="group/code relative my-5 overflow-hidden rounded-2xl bg-(--code-background) text-(--code-foreground)"><pre class="language-jsx overflow-x-auto scroll-fade-x no-scrollbar p-(--pad) font-mono text-sm"><code class="language-jsx code-highlight"><span class="code-line"><span class="token tag"><span class="token tag"><span class="token punctuation">&lt;</span>shaderMaterial</span>
